many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy and practice I've created  this series to provide short answers to  the most common recurring or confusing  questions about stoicism this is an  excellent and commonly arise in question  is the philosophy of stoicism  self-centered and what they mean by that  really is is stoic ethics self-centered  the simple answer to this question is  that yes stoic philosophy is indeed  self-centered but and that's a very  important but it's not self-centered in  the way that most people use that term  to the exclusion of taking into account  or caring about or doing one's duties  towards other people it's understandable  that that answer may seem confusing or  even paradoxical to many people in our  culture because they have this  assumption and it is just an assumption  it's not you know something that you  automatically have to believe in that  either you are focusing on the self and  you're taking care of the self or you  are focusing on others this is the  age-old clash between egoism and  altruism and when it comes to ancient  moral theories the Stoics are very  similar to to some of the other key  moral theories in saying that this is a  false dichotomy at least in many cases  when it's elevated as an either/or  that's a sign of a lack of human moral  development  so the Stoics will say that yes for many  people either you can focus on the self  or focus on the other and because you  focus on the self you're going to find  yourself in conflicts with others but as  a human being  develops as a human being becomes  conscious of what it is not only to be  be rational but more specifically to be  to be just to be distinctively humane  you're going to inevitably widen your  scope  of concern to take in others this is a  doctrine that the Stoics called a OSIS  meaning bringing everybody into one  common community or one household one  boy goes if you like and it's viewed as  a natural development of the human being  who develops without the sorts of  impediments that that draw them into the  wrong path so you begin as a baby very  self-centered you're interested in your  own needs and your requirements and you  cry and you expect other people to take  care of you and that's okay but as you  expand as you take in more as you just  develop your distinctively rational  human nature then you start to take into  account other people and actually feel  affection towards them and recognize  duties towards them and the Stoics think  that it begins within the household but  it can be expanded outward in  ever-growing circles is the way that  here achlys the stoic but also Cicero  who is not a stoic but is writing about  stoic ethics in on duties this is the  way that they picture it these circles  expanding them outward to take in more  and more people until you finally take  in the entirety of humanity and people  that you don't know on some foreign  continent if you run into them Cicero  says well don't deny them water you know  if you can feed them feed them so you  know you begin with self-centeredness  and then you move as you develop as a  human being or as a stoic towards taking  into account other people so why would  people have the idea  specifically with stoicism that it is  self-centered I think there's two main  reasons I mean the Stoics themselves say  that we ought to be concerned with  ourselves and that every being you know  does want to preserve its own life bring  itself to flourishing and so it's quite  natural that we would think that there  is a focus on the self if you go a  little bit further  to stoic philosophy you'll even see the  Stoics talking about a continual  perception of one's own body of one's  own self by each individual being so so  that makes perfect sense the other thing  I think particularly contributes to it  is a misunderstanding of the famous  dichotomy of control  found in epictetus where Epictetus says  listen some things are up to you they're  within your purview they are you know  literally upon you at whom own they are  in your control in your powers how we  translate it other things are not and  those things that are in your control  your own thoughts your opinions your  desires and aversions your choices those  sorts of things are up to you all the  other things you know wealth bodily  health you know social status what other  people think how other people respond to  you that's not up to you that doesn't  mean that the stoic thinks that you  simply disengage yourself from  everything else including other people  as a matter of fact if you read  epictetus you'll see him very you know  clearly saying you actually have duties  towards those other people and that is  up to you so that falls on this side of  the night the academy of control  and how we use external things that's  also up to us and those external things  could be our relationships with other  people so you know a proper  understanding of the dichotomy of  control doesn't isolate the person out  and say screw everybody else they can  all sort their own business out I'm  gonna focus just on myself as a matter  of fact if you're going to develop as a  stoic you should find yourself being  more and more involved with other people  not in a pathological way where their  own problems their own mistaken views on  things their suffering ends up you know  infecting your own and drawing you into  you know their their circus if you like  if we want to use that not my monkeys  not my circus metaphor but in such a way  that you  can in fact properly respond to them  when you find them suffering so  Epictetus will say you know commiserate  with the person who has had a loss just  don't groan inwardly that is being  other-centered but in a way that's also  compatible with being self-centered so  the Stoics are indeed self-centered and  advocating a certain kind of  self-centeredness but it's one that's  very different than what we typically  mean by that term whether we're  deploring self-centeredness and others  or we're justifying a kind of  exaggerated self-centeredness so that we  can have what it is that we want and not  care about others  the Stoics would say that that  understanding of self-centeredness is  actually a deficient understanding of it  and reflective of a poorly developed  character on the part of both people  what the Stoics are advocating is a  truly rational self-centeredness one  that that takes into account others  because our rational nature when it  develops has us engaging with and caring  about and taking care of other people if  you found this answer helpful for you  then please share it with others who  might benefit from it this work is  entirely supported by crowdfunding on my  patreon site so if you find it valuable  consider becoming a supporter at  patreon.com slash Saddler
 

